WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:29.380
I want to make a quick video on the usage
of aspect ratios as a thematic device in film.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:33.440
While the first 40 years of film were limited
to one ratio for length and width of a screen,

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.540
modern filmmakers have several aspect ratios
to choose from, and each one can have different

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.640
emotional effect for the viewer. Let’s look
at why filmmakers choose the ratios they choose

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:50.769
and explore the emotional effect of changing
aspect ratio during a film.

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:55.580
The oldest aspect ratio is the 4:3 Academy
Ratio, and it lost its popularity after the

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.619
introduction of 16:9 ratio in the 1950’s.
Directors still use this ratio sometimes,

00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:04.290
though, to make the film feel like it’s
from an older time period. A great example

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.790
is The Grand Budapest Hotel which switches
to Academy when the story goes back to the 1930's**

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.640
The aspect ratio change is a great
way to emotionally transport us to the time

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.400
period when this ratio was popular. We associate
the aspect ratio with old timey movies, and

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.420
it helps pull us into the old timey style,
quirkiness, manners, and culture that The

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:29.560
Grand Budapest Hotel has.
500 Days of Summer uses Polaroid square ratio

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.670
as a way to show a glamourized, reminiscent
past.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.960
This time capsule look coincides nicely with Tom’s

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.780
old fashioned, unrealistic view of love. Towards
the end of the movie, though, this Polaroid

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.560
look is eventually pushed off screen as Tom
realizes the expectation of this old-fashioned

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:54.020
outlook does not coincide with reality.
2.35 ratio is ultra widescreen, and it gives

00:01:54.020 --> 00:02:00.280
the look of an epic big budget drama or sci-fi film. I like how Aunty Donna uses it for over-the-top drama.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.080
The ratio is reserved for extremely epic movies. A movie like Napoleon Dynamite would look

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.310
kind of off in this extreme ratio, but a movie
like Star Wars wouldn’t look nearly the

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.360
same without it.
Portraying space is commonly associated with

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.310
big aspect ratio changes. Interstellar switches
to IMAX footage when the shots are of space,

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.620
and goes back to 16:9 for shots of the spaceship.
What better way to make space look bigger

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.639
than by literally adding to the screen? The
same is done in Apollo 13 and Galaxy Quest

00:02:38.639 --> 00:02:40.579
to show the huge scope of space.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.200
Aspect ratio changes to add scope aren’t

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.150
exclusive to space movies, though. Right before
the Bane vs. Batman fight in The Dark Knight

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.989
Rises, for example, the camera transitions
to bigger IMAX footage. This change has a

00:02:56.989 --> 00:03:01.260
great subconscious emotional effect. It reminds
us subtly that we’re about to watch an intense,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.400
epic scene. Hunger Games also does a good
job of adding anticipation by slowly expanding

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.659
into IMAX footage before the fighting begins.
The right Aspect Ratio can contribute to the

00:03:09.659 --> 00:03:13.560
realism of a film. A scripted film that’s
supposed to look like a documentary has to

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.859
be 16:9 or else it won’t emotionally feel
like a documentary. The opening scene in The

00:03:17.859 --> 00:03:23.230
Incredibles purposefully looks like 16m 4:3*
ratio film, and the effect adds realism, so

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.529
the movie feels less like a cartoon and more
like a realistic action film.

00:03:26.529 --> 00:03:31.139
Catch Me If You Can cleverly uses aspect ratio
change to show Frank is contained. He starts

00:03:31.139 --> 00:03:35.159
on TV in the small Academy Ratio, and then
he’s next shown through a small slit in

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:38.719
a wall. He is literally being squished in
the frame. It gives the emotionally feeling

00:03:38.719 --> 00:03:41.700
that Frank is trapped.
The most ambitious aspect ratio changes are

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.749
when the ratio is ignored completely. Life
of Pi has the fish literally jumping out of

00:03:45.749 --> 00:03:54.389
the frame, and Oz the Great and Powerful does
the same with a burst of flame. It’s a really

00:03:54.389 --> 00:03:57.079
immersive technique and makes for a great
visual.

00:03:57.079 --> 00:04:00.760
Aspect Ratios have a chance to greatly contribute
to the visual storytelling of a film. Whether

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.329
they indicate time period, intensity, or added
realism, aspect ratios are another clever

00:04:05.329 --> 00:04:09.079
tool at the disposal of the modern director.
If you want to fully understand the style

00:04:09.079 --> 00:04:13.579
of a film, the film’s aspect ratio is a
great frame of reference. Thanks for watching.

